WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:02.984
Almost six thousand Korean words.

00:00:03.809 --> 00:00:05.276
How do you keep them in your head?

00:00:06.347 --> 00:00:11.875
K-Talim is a site for learning Korean, explained in Uzbek, English or Russian.

00:00:12.453 --> 00:00:21.314
Every day starts with a plan: reviews, new words, grammar, exam practice, reading. And a daily goal that you set yourself.

00:00:22.114 --> 00:00:29.811
The words are grouped into topics and levels, from beginner to TOPIK six, and every group keeps its own statistics.

00:00:30.504 --> 00:00:35.886
New words come in short steps. See it. Hear it. Check the meaning. Then answer.

00:00:36.606 --> 00:00:42.689
Then come the flashcards. How well did you remember? Again, hard, good, or easy.

00:00:42.739 --> 00:00:46.800
And look: every button already shows when the card will come back.

00:00:47.538 --> 00:00:50.956
Press Good, and the card is scheduled right on the device.

00:00:51.006 --> 00:00:59.347
Then the progress is saved to the account. There, database triggers check the points, and update the leaderboard and the weekly league.

00:01:02.284 --> 00:01:15.880
But how does it know when? Behind the buttons is FSRS five, the scheduler that Anki also uses. Every card has a stability: the number of days until your chance of remembering falls to ninety percent.

00:01:15.930 --> 00:01:23.692
Answer Good, and the gap grows: four days. Fourteen. Forty-four. Forget it once, and it drops back to about four.

00:01:24.403 --> 00:01:30.193
Looking for a word? Search in Korean, by its meaning, or just by how it sounds.

00:01:31.013 --> 00:01:42.671
Romanized Korean is spelled in many ways. So both sides are folded down to one rough key. G becomes K. Double letters become one. And suddenly, two spellings meet.

00:01:43.470 --> 00:01:48.741
Each grammar pattern has examples with audio, and the mistakes that learners often make.

00:01:49.318 --> 00:01:52.651
Or write a sentence of your own, and let the AI check it.

00:01:52.701 --> 00:01:59.953
The server takes only a signed-in learner, cuts the text to two hundred characters, and spends one check from the daily limit.

00:02:00.003 --> 00:02:09.433
And the model has to answer in a fixed form. Correct, or not? The corrected sentence, an explanation, a translation. All kept in the account.

00:02:13.216 --> 00:02:21.074
Stories are built from the words of a topic. Tap a word, show a translation, listen, and answer questions at the end.

00:02:21.860 --> 00:02:29.075
Exam practice runs against the clock, or one question type at a time, with an explanation after every answer.

00:02:29.742 --> 00:02:38.172
And this is a full reading paper. Forty questions, a timer, flags for later, translations, and the page of the original paper.

00:02:38.894 --> 00:02:47.680
Every answer is saved on the device at once. A few seconds later it goes to the account, so the attempt is there, on every device.

00:02:47.730 --> 00:02:52.487
Started as a guest? The attempts move into the account when you sign in.

00:02:55.419 --> 00:02:58.288
Reading is one thing. But what about speaking?

00:02:59.233 --> 00:03:06.789
This is a real conversation with Friday, the AI partner. The learner chooses a level, and Friday opens with a question.

00:03:06.839 --> 00:03:16.083
Here, a recorded Korean voice is played into the microphone. The sentence appears as text, Friday answers it, and asks the next one.

00:03:16.775 --> 00:03:25.907
So what happens to one sentence? The server checks who is asking, makes sure that nobody sends too much, and looks at how many seconds are left for today.

00:03:25.957 --> 00:03:34.459
Only then does the AI hear the recording. The time is added after the reply. And if the first AI fails, a second one takes over.

00:03:36.352 --> 00:03:44.246
And the counter? It works in one single step. If two requests arrive together, only one of them can take the last place.

00:03:44.996 --> 00:03:52.940
But you can talk to real people, too. These are two test accounts. Each chooses a level, and who to talk to, and waits for a partner.

00:03:53.640 --> 00:04:05.694
First, the database checks the profile, the age, bans, and the time left today. Then it takes the learner who has waited longest, at the same level, if neither has blocked the other.

00:04:05.744 --> 00:04:11.769
That row stays locked while it’s taken. So one person is never given to two callers.

00:04:16.012 --> 00:04:23.921
Both browsers say hello at a meeting point, and agree on how to connect. And then the voice goes directly, from one browser to the other.

00:04:23.971 --> 00:04:28.933
Calls are recorded and kept for thirty days, so that a report can be checked.

00:04:29.742 --> 00:04:41.999
Now, could somebody cheat? Progress is saved from the device, so a database trigger trims what no person could earn. Three points a second, bursts of up to three hundred, three thousand a day.

00:04:42.708 --> 00:04:51.597
And if two devices hold different progress? They’re merged. Each word keeps its most recent record, and counters keep the larger value.

00:04:52.369 --> 00:04:58.210
And every evening at eight, a job finds learners whose streak ends tonight, and reminds them.

00:04:59.487 --> 00:05:09.359
What about Premium? It’s a date on the account, and only the server can set it. It raises the daily limits for AI checks, the tutor, and speaking.

00:05:09.409 --> 00:05:13.022
The limits are settings, so they change without a release.

00:05:14.742 --> 00:05:20.093
Statistics count streaks, accuracy, study time, and how many words are mastered.

00:05:20.843 --> 00:05:24.261
Thirty-seven badges, and levels, mark the way.

00:05:24.911 --> 00:05:32.431
Then there’s the weekly league: groups of up to thirty learners, and the top five move up on Monday. The names are hidden here.

00:05:33.047 --> 00:05:41.598
Groups are filled under a lock, one learner after another. So two people who join together can’t both take the last place.

00:05:42.199 --> 00:05:50.416
Study in Korea lists three hundred and sixty-seven universities, with certification, tuition, scholarships, and visa steps.

00:05:51.097 --> 00:06:02.499
And the AI advisor? It gets only this catalogue and official facts. If the answer isn’t there, it has to say so. And universities that aren’t in the catalogue are dropped from the reply.

00:06:04.318 --> 00:06:16.801
Now, behind the curtain: the admin panel. Seven hundred and ninety-four words in thirty-two topics, plus the official TOPIK word lists. And twenty-three exam papers, with almost two thousand questions.

00:06:17.538 --> 00:06:23.614
A word’s meanings, romanization and example can be corrected here, in three languages.

00:06:24.403 --> 00:06:34.489
Before an edited story or grammar page is saved, its old version goes into the history, so it can be brought back. And learners get the change without a new release.

00:06:35.081 --> 00:06:43.447
The user list has filters for activity, Premium, blocks, and deletion requests. Personal data is blurred here.

00:06:44.233 --> 00:06:47.341
Now, the honest part. Some things are still limited.

00:06:47.391 --> 00:06:52.848
The AI partner has a daily allowance: ten minutes free, two hours with Premium.

00:06:52.898 --> 00:07:02.674
Only two hundred and seventy-eight words are read by real people. The rest use AI or browser voices, so the pronunciation can sound unnatural.

00:07:02.724 --> 00:07:06.362
And Friday runs on light, low-cost AI models.

00:07:06.412 --> 00:07:18.580
With extra budget, that changes. A professional Korean voice for every word and story. Stronger AI, for more natural speech and better corrections. And bigger daily allowances.

00:07:20.081 --> 00:07:22.389
It’s live, at k-talim dot u z.
